  { q: "Is it safe to bring children to Lhasa?", a: "Yes, with proper preparation. Thousands of families visit Lhasa every year. The main concern is altitude, not safety. Lhasa is a peaceful city with very low crime rates. The key is thorough pre-trip planning around altitude acclimatization." },
  { q: "What is the minimum age for children to visit Potala Palace?", a: "There is no official minimum age. However, the climb is steep with many stairs, and children under 5 may find it exhausting at 3,700m. Consider a baby carrier for toddlers. Strollers are not practical inside the palace due to staircases." },
  { q: "Do children need a Tibet Travel Permit?", a: "Yes. Every foreign traveler regardless of age needs a Tibet Travel Permit. Children's permits are processed through the same travel agency as the parents. You will need the child's passport and Chinese visa." },
  { q: "How do I manage altitude sickness with kids?", a: "Children acclimatize similarly to adults but may not be able to articulate symptoms. Watch for unusual tiredness, loss of appetite, or irritability. Keep them hydrated. Plan for 2-3 acclimatization days in Lhasa before any strenuous activity. Consult a pediatrician experienced in high-altitude travel before your trip." },
